    text: "The High Court at Calcutta considered a writ petition filed by Shrimati Shubra Das and another against the Union of India. The petition arose out of a judgment and order passed by the Central Administrative Tribunal, Kolkata Bench, in Original Application No. 738 of 2023. The petitioners challenged the decision concerning the service status and pensionary benefits of their predecessor, Dr. Dhurjati Prasad Das, who had served as a scientist under the Indian Council of Agricultural Research. Dr. Das joined the service of ICAR on 28 September 1976. He was granted study leave from 28 September 1981 to 27 September 1982, which was subsequently extended for another year. He later sought further leave, but the request was refused. From 1 January 1986 to 22 June 1986, he was treated as absent from duty without authorization and was awarded the penalty of censure. He again remained absent from 21 November 1987 to 15 November 1990 without sanctioned leave. Consequently, disciplinary proceedings were initiated and the period of absence was treated as dies non. He was subsequently transferred to Sambalpur. During proceedings challenging the treatment of his absence as dies non, the competent authority issued an office order dated 23 April 2003 deeming Dr. Das to have resigned from service with effect from 29 December 1991 on account of continuous absence. A representation seeking recall of the order was made, but the competent authority confirmed the decision. The original application was dismissed by the Tribunal on 30 November 2004. The matter was then taken before the High Court, which dismissed the writ petition on 15 May 2007. Although an observation that Dr. Das had abandoned his service was subsequently expunged in review, the decision concerning dies non remained undisturbed. The petitioners argued that the office order dated 23 April 2003 did not specify the statutory provision under which it had been issued. They contended that the provision relating to deemed resignation was introduced in the relevant leave rules only at a later stage and therefore could not have been applied in 2003. It was also argued that the President of ICAR was the appointing and disciplinary authority and that another authority could not have issued the order. According to the petitioners, the proper course would have been to initiate disciplinary proceedings under the applicable rules. They relied upon decisions of the Supreme Court concerning the right to a reasonable opportunity of hearing and the necessity of considering whether an absence was wilful or caused by compelling circumstances. The respondents opposed the petition and submitted that the service of Dr. Das was governed by the rules and regulations applicable to ICAR. They pointed out that the relevant rules had already contained a restriction against granting leave for a continuous period exceeding five years, whereas the period of absence in the case exceeded that limit. They further contended that the issue of dies non had already been decided in the earlier round of litigation and that Dr. Das had been given several opportunities to explain his absence and improve his conduct. The Court examined the statutory provisions and the history of the litigation. It noted that although the provisions concerning deemed resignation were substituted in the relevant rules at a later stage, the restriction concerning continuous leave beyond five years was already in existence. The continuous absence of Dr. Das exceeded that period. The Court also found that the competent authority had issued the orders under challenge and had recorded reasons for its decision. The challenge based upon the office memorandum concerning administrative authority was therefore found to have no merit. The Court considered the judgments relied upon by the petitioners concerning the right of hearing and wilful absence. It agreed with the general principles laid down in those decisions but found them inapplicable to the facts because Dr. Das had been granted several opportunities to explain his conduct. The record clearly indicated prolonged absence from duty without sufficient explanation. The Court also held that the principle of res judicata did not apply because the cause of action in the proceedings was different from that in the earlier cases. A decision dealing with actual resignation was also distinguished because the present matter concerned deemed resignation. In conclusion, the Court held that Dr. Das had remained absent from his duties for a prolonged period without adequate explanation despite being given several opportunities. There was no restraint preventing him from performing his duties, and the earlier judicial and administrative proceedings had already considered his conduct. In such circumstances, the Court held that he could not claim pensionary benefits as a matter of right without performing the duties attached to his service. The writ petition was accordingly dismissed without any order as to costs, and the judgment and order of the Central Administrative Tribunal were upheld."
