    wordCount: 786,
    text: "The High Court of Judicature at Bombay considered a Bail Application filed by Akshay Balu Sathe against the State of Maharashtra. The application was made under Section 483 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023, in connection with Crime No. 363 of 2022 registered at Walchandnagar Police Station, District Pune. The applicant was facing an offence punishable under Section 302 of the Indian Penal Code. The matter was heard by the High Court. The applicant had been in custody since 25 August 2022 and had remained incarcerated for more than four years. Although the trial had commenced, only one witness had been examined. After hearing the advocate for the applicant and the learned Additional Public Prosecutor for the State, the Court observed that making any prima facie expression on the merits of the case while the trial was continuing could affect the proceedings. The Court therefore considered the application primarily from the perspective of prolonged incarceration and the applicant's fundamental right to a speedy trial. While considering bail, the Court noted that the principal rule is to ascertain whether the accused is likely to appear before the Court for trial. Other relevant factors include the gravity of the offence, the possibility of the accused repeating the offence, the likelihood of influencing witnesses or tampering with evidence, and the antecedents of the accused. The Court also noticed the problem of prolonged trials. The Court emphasized that the case involved the personal liberty of an under-trial prisoner and engaged Article 21 of the Constitution of India. Where an accused has remained incarcerated for a very long period while the trial is progressing slowly, the High Court possesses wide powers to consider release on bail. The established principle is that bail is the rule and refusal is the exception. The Court referred to earlier judicial decisions which recognized that an accused who is free is in a better position to prepare and conduct his defence than one who remains in custody. The Court relied upon the principles explained by the Supreme Court in Satender Kumar Antil v. Central Bureau of Investigation. It noted that a large proportion of persons in Indian prisons are under-trial prisoners and that arrest and detention should not be treated as punishment before conviction. Bail is a conditional release intended to secure the presence of the accused and cooperation with the investigation and trial. The Court also referred to the principle that the object of bail is neither punitive nor preventative. Deprivation of liberty before conviction must be justified by the necessity of securing the accused's appearance at trial. The Court further considered the presumption of innocence and the protection of life and personal liberty under Article 21. The procedure for depriving a person of liberty must be fair, just and reasonable. The Court referred to Hussainara Khatoon v. Home Secretary, State of Bihar, which recognized speedy trial as an integral and essential part of the fundamental right to life and liberty. It also considered decisions emphasizing that prolonged incarceration may justify bail where the trial is unlikely to be completed within a reasonable period. The Court referred to Abdul Rehman Antulay v. R.S. Nayak and noted that the right to speedy trial extends through the stages of investigation, inquiry, trial, appeal, revision and retrial. From the viewpoint of an accused, pre-conviction detention should be as short as possible. Prolonged proceedings can cause anxiety, expense and disturbance to personal life, and delay may also impair the ability of an accused to defend himself because witnesses may die, disappear or become unavailable. In the present case, the applicant had remained in custody for more than four years and there was no distinct possibility of the trial concluding in the near future. Only one witness had been examined and the pace of the trial was slow. Considering the prolonged incarceration and the right to speedy trial, the Court allowed the Bail Application. The applicant was directed to be released on furnishing a personal recognizance bond of Rs. 25,000 with one or two sureties in the like amount. He was required to report to the Investigating Officer once every month, cooperate with the trial and attend the Trial Court on all dates unless specifically exempted. The Court further directed that the applicant should not leave the State of Maharashtra without prior permission, should not influence witnesses or tamper with evidence, and should keep the Investigating Officer informed of his current address and mobile contact details. Any violation of the conditions could result in cancellation of bail. The Court clarified that its observations were prima facie and would not affect the merits of the ongoing trial. The Bail Application was accordingly allowed and disposed of on the stated terms."
