    text: "The High Court of Judicature at Bombay considered Bail Application No. 2197 of 2026 filed by Ganesh Bhausaheb Kale against the State of Maharashtra. The application was made under Section 439 of the Criminal Procedure Code seeking regular bail in connection with C.R. No. 37 of 2021 registered with Gangapur Police Station, District Nashik. The applicant faced offences under Sections 302, 120B, 115, 201, 109, 419, 420, 465, 466, 467, 468 and 471 read with Section 34 of the Indian Penal Code. Provisions of the Maharashtra Control of Organised Crime Act, 1999 were also invoked. The applicant had been incarcerated for more than five years and six months. The First Information Report had been registered on 18 February 2021, and the applicant was arraigned as Accused No. 10. The main accused, namely the gang leader arraigned as Accused No. 1, had already been released on bail. The Court prima facie found that the role attributed to the applicant was at par with other co-accused persons who had also been released on bail. The applicant therefore relied upon the principle of parity as an important ground for seeking his release. The Advocate for the applicant placed before the Court an order of the Supreme Court dated 24 February 2026, by which another co-accused having an identical role had been released on bail on the ground of long incarceration. The prosecution was expected to examine 119 witnesses and the trial was being dragged on. Considering the Supreme Court order and the fact that the applicant had remained in custody for more than five years and six months, the Court found that the applicant had made out a clear case on the ground of parity with Accused No. 8. It was also noted that no motive had been attributed to the present applicant. While deciding a Bail Application, the Court has to consider whether the accused is likely to appear before the Court for trial. Other broad parameters include the gravity of the offence, the likelihood of the accused repeating the offence while on bail, the possibility of influencing witnesses or tampering with evidence, and the antecedents of the accused. The Court observed that the material available while deciding a bail application is limited. It also noted the serious problem of prolonged trials and overcrowding in prisons. The Court referred to Article 21 of the Constitution and the rights of an under-trial to speedy justice and personal liberty. Where an under-trial has remained incarcerated for a very long period pending trial, the powers of the High Court to grant bail are wide. The established principle is that bail is the rule and refusal is the exception. Judicial decisions have explained that an accused person, being presumed innocent until guilt is proved, should have an opportunity to prepare and defend the case. Detention during trial is not intended to operate as punishment. The judgment referred to important decisions including Satender Kumar Antil v. Central Bureau of Investigation, Gudikanti Narasimhulu v. Public Prosecutor, Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India, Hussainara Khatoon v. Home Secretary, State of Bihar, and Union of India v. K. A. Najeeb. These decisions emphasize that personal liberty is protected by Article 21 and that a reasonably expeditious trial is an important part of that constitutional guarantee. Where incarceration is prolonged and the trial is unlikely to conclude soon, constitutional courts may consider release on bail. The Court also discussed the right to speedy trial in Abdul Rehman Antulay v. R.S. Nayak. The right extends to investigation, inquiry, trial, appeal, revision and retrial. From the perspective of an accused, the period of remand and pre-conviction detention should be as short as possible. Undue delay may cause worry, anxiety, expense and disturbance to the accused and may impair the ability to defend the case if witnesses die, disappear or become unavailable. In the present case, the applicant had remained in custody for five years and six months, and there was no possibility of the trial concluding in the near future. The Court found that the applicant had made out a case for grant of bail on the grounds of long incarceration as well as parity. Provisional cash bail was granted because there was nobody in his family who could arrange the surety and bail amount. He was directed to furnish provisional cash bail of Rs. 25,000 and to file an undertaking within one week stating that he would provide one or two sureties in the like amount within four weeks after release."
