    text: "The High Court of Judicature at Calcutta considered WPA 17939 of 2026 filed by Manas Mandal against the Union of India and others. The writ petition challenged the transfer of the petitioner effected by an order dated July 13, 2026. The judgment was delivered by the Hon’ble Mr. Justice Reetobroto Kumar Mitra on September 9, 2026. The petitioner had been appointed as Chief Engineer on September 12, 2012 by the Haldia Dock Complex under Kolkata Port Trust and was later working as Chief Mechanical Engineer at Mumbai Port. In 2022, the petitioner underwent major cardiac surgery and requested a home posting in Kolkata. His request was initially declined. On March 16, 2024, he was transferred to the Haldia Dock Complex, where he joined as General Manager (Engineering) on March 23, 2024. Subsequently, the authority introduced a new transfer policy for heads of departments at major port trusts through a notification dated July 29, 2024. On July 13, 2026, the petitioner was transferred to Cochin under the new policy. He made a demand for justice on July 17, 2026, but received no response. He later learned that the post he was relieving at Haldia Dock Complex was being filled through an advertisement for absorption by composite method. The petitioner challenged the transfer on several grounds. He argued that the order was contrary to the transfer policy because Clause 7.2 provided for a minimum retention period of five years. Since he had served at his present post for only about two years and four months, he claimed that the transfer was contrary to the policy. He also argued that his frequent transfers could not be justified by his work, as there appeared to be no complaint regarding the quality of his performance. Considering his fragile health and the medical treatment undergone in 2022, he submitted that continuous medical attention was more easily available in his home State, West Bengal. He further alleged that the transfer was mala fide and pointed out that his existing post was being advertised for absorption. The Union of India opposed the petition and explained that the transfer policy contained three distinct categories of transfer: rotational transfer, transfer on administrative grounds, and transfer on request. According to the Union, the petitioner’s case fell under Clause 7.2b, relating to administrative transfer, and not under Clause 7.2a, which concerned rotational transfer. The five-year period was applicable to rotational transfers and did not prevent an administrative transfer before completion of five years. The Union therefore submitted that the transfer was an internal administrative action and could not be faulted. The Court examined the transfer policy and observed that each category of transfer had its own separate parameters. Clause 7.2a dealt with rotational transfer, Clause 7.2b dealt with transfer on administrative grounds, and Clause 7.2c dealt with transfer on request. The yardstick applicable to one category could not be automatically applied to another. Since the petitioner had been transferred on administrative grounds under Clause 7.2b, the five-year requirement associated with rotational transfer did not apply to his case. The Court further held that an administrative transfer could be made before an officer completed five years in a particular post. Administrative exigencies may require an employer to transfer an incumbent in order to meet operational requirements. Therefore, applying the five-year restriction applicable to rotational transfers to an administrative transfer would create an anomalous situation and would be inconsistent with the transfer policy. The Court also considered the allegation of mala fide. It observed that a transfer order may generally be interfered with only when it violates a mandatory statutory rule or is made on the ground of mala fide. A mere allegation of mala fide, without proper pleading and substantial proof, is insufficient. In the present case, there was nothing on record to establish that the authority had acted with malice or bad faith. The Court therefore rejected this ground of challenge. The judgment referred to decisions of the Supreme Court concerning judicial review of transfer orders. In N.K. Singh, it was observed that interference with a transfer should be rare where the career prospects of the government servant remain unaffected. In S.L. Abbas and Pubi Lombi, the principle was reiterated that courts should not normally interfere with transfers unless the order is mala fide or violates statutory provisions. The Court also referred to Shilpi Bose, which recognizes that a government employee has no vested right to remain posted at a particular place and may be transferred from one place to another. The petitioner’s medical condition was also considered. The Court acknowledged that he had undergone treatment in 2022 and had requested sympathetic consideration."
