    text: "The High Court of Orissa at Cuttack considered a batch of writ petitions filed by employees of the Central Bank of India who had been dismissed from service. The petitions challenged orders passed by the disciplinary authority imposing the punishment of dismissal and the subsequent orders of the appellate authority confirming such punishment. The common judgment was delivered by the Hon’ble Justice Biraja Prasanna Satapathy on September 8, 2026. The dispute mainly concerned the submission of school certificates that were later found to be forged and the question whether dismissal from service was a proportionate punishment. The petitioners had been working as Safai Karmachari-cum-Sub Staff in different branches of the Bank on a daily wage basis. Pursuant to a recruitment notice issued on August 14, 2012, temporary and casual workers were given a one-time opportunity to participate in the recruitment process for regular appointment as Safai Karmachari-cum-Sub Staff or Sub Staff. The notice prescribed several eligibility conditions. A candidate was required to have completed at least 45 days of service during a continuous period of twelve months. The prescribed educational qualification was minimum eighth standard pass, while the normal age limit was between eighteen and twenty-six years, subject to specified relaxations. The petitioners participated in the recruitment process and were selected for regular appointment. At the time of joining, they produced certificates in support of their educational qualification and age. Subsequently, the Bank verified the certificates with the concerned schools. According to the Bank, the schools did not confirm the issuance of the certificates, and disciplinary proceedings were therefore initiated against the employees. The enquiry officers found the charges proved. Show-cause notices proposing dismissal were then issued, and after considering the replies, the disciplinary authority imposed the punishment of dismissal. Appeals filed before the appellate authority were also rejected. The petitioners challenged the punishment before the High Court. Their principal submission was that, although they had committed misconduct by producing incorrect or forged certificates, dismissal was excessively harsh and disproportionate. They relied upon judicial decisions concerning the doctrine of proportionality and argued that punishment should be appropriate to both the nature of the misconduct and the circumstances of the employees. They also pointed out that they had initially worked as daily wage employees and that the recruitment process had offered them an opportunity for regular employment. According to them, a lesser punishment would have been sufficient to meet the ends of justice. The Bank, on the other hand, argued that the employees had deliberately submitted false certificates to obtain regular appointments. It was submitted that proper departmental enquiries had been conducted and that the employees had been given opportunities to defend themselves. Since the misconduct had been established in accordance with the applicable procedure, the Bank maintained that the punishment of dismissal did not call for judicial interference. The Court examined the rival submissions and the principles governing judicial review of disciplinary punishment. It referred to the Supreme Court decision in Ranjit Thakur v. Union of India, which emphasizes that punishment should not be vindictive or unduly harsh and should bear a reasonable relationship to the misconduct. The doctrine of proportionality permits judicial review where a punishment is so disproportionate that it shocks the conscience of the court. The Court also considered the principles stated in B.C. Chaturvedi v. Union of India and State of Gujarat v. Anand Acharya regarding the limited power of a court to interfere with the quantum of punishment. After examining the facts of the present batch, the Court noted that the petitioners were employees who had originally been working on daily wages and had later obtained regular appointments through the recruitment process. The Court accepted that submission of false certificates constituted misconduct. Nevertheless, it considered the punishment of dismissal to be disproportionate when the entire factual background, including the social and educational status of the petitioners and the resulting loss of livelihood, was taken into account. Accordingly, the Court interfered with the impugned orders of dismissal and the orders passed by the appellate authority. The matters were remitted to the appellate authority, namely the Regional Manager of the Central Bank of India, Regional Office, Bhubaneswar, for consideration of an appropriate punishment other than dismissal, removal or compulsory retirement, subject to the circumstances recorded in the judgment. The Court emphasized that disciplinary punishment must be commensurate with the misconduct and that justice requires consideration of all relevant circumstances. All the writ petitions were therefore disposed of with the aforesaid observations and directions."
